**How I counted:**
- **Blame:** I ran `git blame -w` on every tracked file at HEAD, so whitespace-only changes didn't count. This mattered for Troy's old "jslint"/"lint"/"whitespace" commits, which mostly re-indented code.
- **Formatting-only commits skipped:**
  - `62628c0` (Fiona Formatter, the 4-space/double-quote reformat): git couldn't trace 237 lines past this commit because the quotes changed. Its edits replace lines one-for-one, so I traced each of those lines back to the commit before it.
  - `815c7c6` (Linus Unnebäck, restyled the README code examples).
  - `739dc11` (Douglas Christopher Wilson, re-wrapped the LICENSE text).

  After that, no surviving line is credited to any of these three commits.
- **Kept:** `b2005d9` ("lib: annotate handlers") adds `// checked` comments, so it's a content change, not formatting.
- **One person, one count:** Troy Goode commits as both `<email redacted>` and `troy@laptop.example`, so I merged the two (48 lines from the laptop address). I assumed they're the same person because the name matches; please confirm. I also merged `alex94puchades` with Alejandro Caravaca Puchades, since they share an email address.
- **Bots:** I left dependabot's 10 lines out of the totals (in `.github/` and `package.json`).

**Borderline results:**
- **package.json** is at exactly 50%. If dependabot's 2 lines in it counted, Wilson would drop to 47.6% and the file would have no owner.
- **README.md** is at 51.3% only because the Linus style commit is skipped. I didn't re-run the count with that commit included. It holds 37 README lines that would otherwise mostly go to Troy, so Troy would probably fall below half.
- **test/** stays under half for Troy (46.9%) even with both of his addresses merged.

**Check before using:** `@troygoode` and `@dougwilson` are my guesses at their GitHub usernames; git history only records names and emails. Also, a lot of the surviving lines come from people who may no longer be active, so you may want to treat this as a starting point rather than a final assignment.
